WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.377
Now we will combine several NumPy concepts in a small sales project.

00:00:04.377 --> 00:00:08.757
The array contains three product rows and four month columns.

00:00:08.757 --> 00:00:12.437
Before calculating anything, we print the shape.

00:00:12.437 --> 00:00:16.237
That confirms we have three products and four months.

00:00:16.237 --> 00:00:20.727
Then we calculate product totals using sum with axis one.

00:00:20.727 --> 00:00:25.847
Because we want one result for each row, axis one is the correct choice.

00:00:25.847 --> 00:00:30.217
Next, we calculate monthly totals using axis zero.

00:00:30.217 --> 00:00:33.122
This gives one result for each column.

00:00:33.122 --> 00:00:38.102
Finally, argmax identifies the position of the product with the highest total sales.

00:00:38.102 --> 00:00:43.687
Click Run and compare the product totals with the monthly totals.

00:00:43.687 --> 00:00:47.567
This is a good example of why understanding axis matters.

00:00:47.567 --> 00:00:52.577
The same sum function answers two different questions depending on the axis.

00:00:52.577 --> 00:00:57.717
When interpreting the result, remember that argmax returns a row index.

00:00:57.717 --> 00:01:03.245
In a larger project, we would usually connect that index to a product name or identifier.
